WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:05.310
For much of the 1990s, actor and comedian
Martin Lawrence was one of the biggest stars

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:10.250
in Hollywood, with a popular long-running
TV show, a hit stand-up album, and a string

00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:11.520
of box office hits.

00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:16.160
But Lawrence's ascent started to stall toward
the end of the decade, and his star power

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:17.540
has been fading ever since.

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:19.420
So where did he disappear to?

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:22.380
Here's a look at what Martin Lawrence has
been up to over the years.

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:24.380
Early fame

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:29.460
Martin Lawrence began his entertainment career
as a stand-up comedian, but it wasn't long

00:00:29.470 --> 00:00:34.079
before his act caught television producers'
attention, and he made his TV debut on the

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:35.520
series What's Happening Now!.

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:41.440
In 1992, Lawrence was chosen to be the first
host of HBO's Def Comedy Jam, and from there

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:43.620
he found himself on the fast-track to fame.

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:48.380
Not only did he land a role in the Eddie Murphy
comedy Boomerang, he also put his talents

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:52.940
front and center with his very own Fox sitcom,
the aptly titled Martin.

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:57.100
The show proved a major hit with audiences
and made its star a household name.

00:00:57.140 --> 00:01:02.579
That was enough to land him a role opposite
Will Smith in Bad Boys in 1995, and the rest,

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:04.500
as they say, is history.

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:06.260
Meltdown

00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:11.720
On the heels of major box office success,
Lawrence kept himself busy—maybe a little

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:12.720
too busy.

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:18.260
With his first post-Bad Boys role, the actor
set out to reaffirm his leading man status

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:20.580
while proving he could write and direct, too.

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:25.620
But the pressure of carrying a film almost
entirely on his own may have proved too much:

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:30.160
weeks into filming the dark romantic comedy
A Thin Line Between Love And Hate, the actor

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:33.540
had a meltdown on the set and was hospitalized
for exhaustion.

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:36.940
Rather than take some time off, Lawrence continued
to book new work.

00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:42.200
In 1996, he suffered another episode, this
time stalking the streets of L.A. with a pistol

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:43.940
while shouting at passing cars.

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:47.820
His second bout with exhaustion led to another
hospital stay.

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:51.800
This time, Lawrence got the message and stepped
away from the spotlight to heal.

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:53.460
Personal drama

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:58.360
While his professional fortunes skyrocketed,
Lawrence's private life grew turbulent.

00:01:58.610 --> 00:02:03.020
The comedian spent much of the '90s struggling
with personal issues that threatened to derail

00:02:03.020 --> 00:02:03.720
his career.

00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:08.739
He became engaged to Saved By The Bell actress
Lark Voorhies in 1993, but the relationship

00:02:08.740 --> 00:02:10.360
fell apart before year's end.

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:15.860
He rebounded by marrying former Miss Virginia
Patricia Southall in 1995, and the pair had

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:18.540
two children together before divorcing in
1997.

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:20.300
Big screen return

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:25.840
Lawrence eased his way back into Hollywood's
good graces in 1999 with a couple of successful

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:30.629
comedies in Life and Blue Streak—but he
also found his way back into the tabloids

00:02:30.629 --> 00:02:33.260
while prepping for his next film, Big Momma's
House.

00:02:33.380 --> 00:02:38.239
Lawrence collapsed while jogging in 100-degree
heat and ended up in a three-day coma.

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:42.900
"And I just picked the hottest day of the
summer next thing I know I was asleep."

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:48.450
The production was delayed while he recovered,
but the time off didn't hurt: when the film

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:53.740
finally arrived in theaters in 2000, it raked
in close to $180 million worldwide.

00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:56.280
Just like that, Martin Lawrence was back on
top.

00:02:56.840 --> 00:02:58.160
Box office flops

00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:03.920
Unfortunately, Lawrence followed his Big Momma
success with a string of genuine misfires.

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:08.840
What's The Worst That Could Happen?, Black
Knight, and National Security all underperformed,

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:13.680
and in spite of solid box office returns for
Bad Boys II and a Big Momma's House sequel,

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:16.020
he hasn't managed to right the ship for good.

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:20.120
With his movies continuing to miss the mark,
Lawrence tried to return to the medium he

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:21.740
once dominated: television.

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:26.860
Unfortunately, TV has proven a struggle as
well, as Lawrence has seen networks pass on

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:27.600
his pilots.

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:33.440
And the one series that did get picked up— Partners— lasted
just 10 episodes before getting the axe.

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:35.440
Bad Boys bust

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:40.320
No role seemed better suited to get Lawrence
back on track than a return to his big-screen

00:03:40.330 --> 00:03:42.430
breakout as Marcus from Bad Boys.

00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:47.820
"Now back up, put the gun down, and gimmie
a pack of Tropical Fruit Bubbalicious."

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:50.220
"And some Skittles."

00:03:50.900 --> 00:03:56.660
After an extended period in development limbo,
Bad Boys III—a.k.a. Bad Boys For Life—got

00:03:56.660 --> 00:03:59.880
some good news when writer/director Joe Carnahan
came aboard.

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:04.540
"It's real, they workin' on the script and
it it all looks good."

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:09.280
But just as things got cooking, the pieces
holding the sequel together fell apart.

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:13.360
Carnahan left the project, and the studio
eventually pulled the film from its release

00:04:13.380 --> 00:04:13.880
slate.

00:04:13.960 --> 00:04:18.060
Like that, Lawrence's big-screen comeback
was over before it even began.

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:19.780
Semi-comeback

00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:24.500
Lawrence decided to take his talents back
to the stand-up stage in 2016.

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:29.340
Martin Lawrence: Doin' Time premiered on Showtime
and marked his first stand-up film in almost

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:30.100
15 years.

00:04:30.340 --> 00:04:33.460
Sadly, the Showtime special didn't put Lawrence
back in the spotlight.

00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:37.880
Doin' Time arrived with little fanfare, and
was met with critical indifference—but as

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:41.720
with any other creative venture, there's also
the possibility that he just needs to get

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:42.580
warmed up again.

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:46.280
It's always possible that we'll be seeing
a lot more of Martin Lawrence in the future.

00:04:46.520 --> 00:04:47.500
"Thank you very much."

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:52.540
"Break it down!"

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:53.760
Thanks for watching!

